@*
    Every key the schema does not define, drawn as itself.

    The convention says not to invent fields, and this is what happens when one is
    invented anyway — or, far more usefully, when the schema genuinely grows and
    this library has not caught up. A reader that dropped an unknown key would make
    that addition invisible: the field would be in the file, absent from the view,
    and nobody could tell which of the two was wrong.

    It is also where a `related` entry that will not parse as an address lands,
    under the relation it was written on. A typo is only useful once it is visible.

    The values are plain strings and stay plain strings. Nothing here knows what an
    unrecognised key means, so nothing here may promise a destination for it — the
    quiet value treatment is the honest one.

    Rows, and not a value cell like the other field components. How many rows there
    are is a property of the file rather than of the schema, and each row's label is
    a key nobody knew about, so the record cannot lay them out in advance.

    A key the reader recognises and deliberately drops never arrives here. `order`
    is the one of those: see MetadataReader.KnownFields for why moving a field out of
    the schema is not the same as moving it into this list.
*@

@foreach (var (key, values) in Extra)
{
    <MetadataField Label="@key"><MetadataValueList Values="@values" /></MetadataField>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>The unrecognised keys and their values, in the order the reader
    /// collected them. Empty draws nothing at all.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, IReadOnlyList<string>> Extra { get; set; } =
        new Dictionary<string, IReadOnlyList<string>>();
}
